questions.explanation:
HC \equiv C-CH_{{2}}-{\overset{\text{OH}}{\text{CH}}}-CH_{{2}}-CH=CH_{{2}}
{\underset{7}{\text{HC}}} \equiv {\underset{6}{\text{C}}}-{\underset{5}{\text{CH}}}_{{2}}-{\underset{4}{\overset{\text{OH}}{\text{CH}}}}-{\underset{3}{\text{CH}}}_{{2}}-{\underset{2}{\text{CH}}} = {\underset{1}{\text{CH}}}_{{2}}
The longest carbon chain contains 7 carbons (hept). The functional groups are a hydroxyl group (ol), a double bond (ene), and a triple bond (yne). When numbering the chain, the principal functional group (hydroxyl) should have the lowest possible locant. If there's a tie in locants for multiple bonds, the double bond gets priority over the triple bond.
Numbering from right to left gives the hydroxyl group at C4, the double bond at C1, and the triple bond at C6. This gives the locant set (1, 4, 6) for the multiple bonds and the hydroxyl group.
Numbering from left to right would give the hydroxyl group at C4, the triple bond at C1, and the double bond at C6. This also gives the locant set (1, 4, 6). However, when there is a tie in locants for multiple bonds, the double bond is given priority for the lower number. Therefore, numbering from right to left is correct.
The correct IUPAC name is Hept-1-en-6-yn-4-ol.

Given reaction:
A_{(g)} \rightleftharpoons B_{(g)} + \frac{1}{2}C_{(g)}
Initial moles at \(t=0\):
\begin{array}{cccc}
A_{(g)} & \rightleftharpoons & B_{(g)} & + \frac{1}{2}C_{(g)} \\
n & & - & - \\
\end{array}
Moles at equilibrium \(t=eq\):
\begin{array}{cccc}
A_{(g)} & \rightleftharpoons & B_{(g)} & + \frac{1}{2}C_{(g)} \\
n(1-\alpha) & & n\alpha & n\alpha/2 \\
\end{array}
Total moles at equilibrium:
\text{total moles} = n(1-\alpha) + n\alpha + n\alpha/2 = n(1 + \alpha/2)
The expression for \(K_p\) is:
K_p = \frac{(P_B)(P_C)^{1/2}}{(P_A)}
Where partial pressures are given by:
P_A = \frac{n(1-\alpha)}{n(1+\alpha/2)} \times P = \frac{1-\alpha}{1+\alpha/2} P
P_B = \frac{n\alpha}{n(1+\alpha/2)} \times P = \frac{\alpha}{1+\alpha/2} P
P_C = \frac{n\alpha/2}{n(1+\alpha/2)} \times P = \frac{\alpha/2}{1+\alpha/2} P
Substitute these into the \(K_p\) expression:
K_p = \frac{\left(\frac{n\alpha}{n(1+\alpha/2)} \times P\right) \left(\frac{n\alpha/2}{n(1+\alpha/2)} \times P\right)^{1/2}}{\left(\frac{n(1-\alpha)}{n(1+\alpha/2)} \times P\right)}
Simplify the expression:
K_p = \frac{\frac{\alpha P}{1+\alpha/2} \cdot \frac{(\alpha/2)^{1/2} P^{1/2}}{(1+\alpha/2)^{1/2}}}{\frac{(1-\alpha)P}{1+\alpha/2}}
K_p = \frac{\alpha P \cdot \frac{\alpha^{1/2} P^{1/2}}{\sqrt{2}}}{(1+\alpha/2)^{1/2}} \cdot \frac{1}{(1-\alpha)P}
K_p = \frac{\alpha^{3/2} P^{1/2}}{\sqrt{2}(1+\alpha/2)^{1/2}(1-\alpha)}
Since \(1+\alpha/2 = \frac{2+\alpha}{2}\), we have \((1+\alpha/2)^{1/2} = \frac{(2+\alpha)^{1/2}}{\sqrt{2}}\).
Substitute this back:
K_p = \frac{\alpha^{3/2} P^{1/2}}{\sqrt{2} \frac{(2+\alpha)^{1/2}}{\sqrt{2}} (1-\alpha)}
K_p = \frac{\alpha^{3/2}P^{1/2}}{(2+\alpha)^{1/2}(1-\alpha)}
This matches option (1).
